      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by identifying the lengths of the legs of the right triangle. The legs are given as \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\log_4 27\\) can be rewritten using the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_2 4 = 2\\), this simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}.\n\\]\nWe know that \\(27 = 3^3\\), so \\(\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(3 \\log_2 3\\) in a more convenient form. Recall that \\(3 = 2^{\\log_2 3}\\), so:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 3 = 3 \\cdot (\\log_2 3)^2 = 3 \\cdot (\\log_2 3)^2.\n\\]\nHowever, we can simplify this expression by recognizing that we can use the properties of logarithms and exponents directly. We know that:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 (3^3) = \\log_2 27.\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\) using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\) and \\(\\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3\\), we substitute these into the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2 + 16 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{25 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2} = \\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we need to find \\(4^h\\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5 \\log_2 3}{2}} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5 = 243.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}.\n\\]",
